{"author":{"id":"973fc03426eba9a243913e0f6e6823e102397744f60a9ab11c882af948c310c4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1774360454,"nonce":"IoRnhA83c6Sk1vkmRg/h9KgmBl0=","title":"Project-associated server/service management","message":"Add central management of servers and services associated with projects. Similar to how build systems (package.json scripts, Makefile targets) define commands for a package, Mandible should let users associate shell commands with projects:\n\n**Core concept:**\n- `mandible project service add \u003cproject\u003e --name \"web\" --cwd \u003cpath\u003e --command \"npm run dev\"` \n- `mandible project service start \u003cproject\u003e [service-name]`\n- `mandible project service stop \u003cproject\u003e [service-name]`\n- `mandible project service status \u003cproject\u003e`\n\n**Use cases:**\n1. **Dev servers**: Start/stop web app dev servers (Vite, FastAPI) with one command\n2. **Post-merge hooks**: After an auth merge succeeds, automatically restart the affected projects services (similar to how Mandible itself rebuilds after a merge to its protected branch)\n3. **Multi-service projects**: A project like Feedbax has both a Vite frontend and a FastAPI backend — manage both as named services\n4. **Server discovery**: Instead of `lsof -i :3008` and grepping process trees, `mandible project service status feedbax` shows what is running, on what port, from which worktree\n\n**Integration with auth system:**\n- On merge completion, Mandible could check if the merged project has associated services and offer to restart them\n- This would prevent the problem of stale servers running from old worktrees after merges\n\n**Relation to project registry (issue 3d85f43):**\n- Builds on the project name → path resolution from that issue\n- Services are stored per-project in the Mandible database alongside the project registration","files":null}]}